Welcome takes no inputs here and returns a React element describing an h1. return supplies that description, not a finished browser node. <Welcome /> asks React to use the component; its uppercase name distinguishes it from the lowercase HTML tag <h1>. App also takes no inputs here and returns the Welcome element.

```jsx
function Welcome() {
  return <h1>Map desk</h1>;
}
function App() {
  return <Welcome />;
}
```

The screen shows the heading Map desk. Neither function prints to the console.

Changing the text inside Welcome changes the heading to Ticket desk. App can keep the same component use.

```jsx
function Welcome() {
  return <h1>Ticket desk</h1>;
}
function App() {
  return <Welcome />;
}
```

App takes no inputs in this example. It returns a p element whose children include the station string and the result of level + 1. The braces do not print themselves. className supplies the HTML class attribute in JSX; it does not automatically create a CSS rule.

```jsx
function App() {
  const station = "North";
  const level = 3;
  return <p className="signal">{station}: {level + 1}</p>;
}
```

The paragraph reads North: 4 and has the class signal.

The changed constants produce West: 7. The colon and space remain literal text.

```jsx
function App() {
  const station = "West";
  const level = 6;
  return <p className="signal">{station}: {level + 1}</p>;
}
```

Pass receives one props object. props.name reads its name field; Pass returns a paragraph containing that string. In <Pass name="Rowan" />, name is the prop name and Rowan is its value. React calls Pass for each use. App returns a main containing both component descriptions.

```jsx
function Pass(props) {
  return <p>{props.name}</p>;
}
function App() {
  return <main><Pass name="Rowan" /><Pass name="Sage" /></main>;
}
```

The two paragraphs read Rowan and Sage. The component definition is shared, but the input values differ.

Changing only the second name prop makes the paragraphs Rowan and Mira. The first use is not changed.

```jsx
function Pass(props) {
  return <p>{props.name}</p>;
}
function App() {
  return <main><Pass name="Rowan" /><Pass name="Mira" /></main>;
}
```

Panel receives props.children, which is the nested React content. It returns a section containing its own Notice heading followed by that content. Reading props.children does not call the child as a plain function or convert it to a string.

```jsx
function Panel(props) {
  return <section><h2>Notice</h2>{props.children}</section>;
}
function App() {
  return <Panel><p>Water is available.</p></Panel>;
}
```

The section contains the Notice heading and the paragraph Water is available.

The same Panel now contains a View route button instead of the paragraph. Panel itself is unchanged.

```jsx
function Panel(props) {
  return <section><h2>Notice</h2>{props.children}</section>;
}
function App() {
  return <Panel><button>View route</button></Panel>;
}
```

chooseMap takes no arguments in this example and has no explicit return value. Its effect is to replace lastAction. onClick={chooseMap} passes the function itself. onClick={chooseMap()} would call it during render and pass its return value instead. App returns the button without calling chooseMap.

```jsx
let lastAction = "none";
function chooseMap() {
  lastAction = "map chosen";
}
function App() {
  return <button onClick={chooseMap}>Choose map</button>;
}
```

Before a click, lastAction is none. Clicking Choose map makes it map chosen. The button text stays Choose map because changing this ordinary variable does not request a React render.

The changed handler records route chosen when Choose route is clicked. Again, the action is recorded only after the event.

```jsx
let lastAction = "none";
function chooseMap() {
  lastAction = "route chosen";
}
function App() {
  return <button onClick={chooseMap}>Choose route</button>;
}
```

React.useState(2) receives the initial value and returns a two-item array: the current count and a setter function. The bracketed names unpack those two items. addOne is a click handler; setCount(count + 1) supplies the next number. The setter does not return the new count for you to display. Call the Hook at the top of App, not inside the handler or a condition.

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(2);
  function addOne() {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }
  return <main><p>Tickets: {count}</p><button onClick={addOne}>Add ticket</button></main>;
}
```

The first screen says Tickets: 2. One click renders Tickets: 3; a second separate click renders Tickets: 4.

Starting at 5 makes the first screen Tickets: 5 and one click Tickets: 6. The same handler uses the current render’s count.

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(5);
  function addOne() {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }
  return <main><p>Tickets: {count}</p><button onClick={addOne}>Add ticket</button></main>;
}
```

The two useState calls remember a count and a report string separately. record calls setCount with count +1, then calls setReport with a string built from the same old count. Both setters request the next render. The output element displays the report string; it does not perform the calculation itself.

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(1);
  const [report, setReport] = React.useState("Not recorded");
  function record() {
    setCount(count + 1);
    setReport("Read " + count);
  }
  return <main><p>Count: {count}</p><output>{report}</output><button onClick={record}>Record</button></main>;
}
```

After one click, the paragraph says Count: 2 and the output says Read 1. That is not a lost update: the report deliberately records the snapshot read by this event.

With initial count 4, one click produces Count: 5 and Read 4. The setter call still does not replace the handler’s local count.

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(4);
  const [report, setReport] = React.useState("Not recorded");
  function record() {
    setCount(count + 1);
    setReport("Read " + count);
  }
  return <main><p>Count: {count}</p><output>{report}</output><button onClick={record}>Record</button></main>;
}
```

increment receives the previous queued number and returns that number plus 1. It does not change outside data. setCount(increment) passes this function for React to call; the learner passes the function instead of invoking it. React controls when and how often the pure updater runs. Two such calls queue two transformations. App still calls useState at its top level.

```jsx
function increment(previous) {
  return previous + 1;
}
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);
  function addPair() {
    setCount(increment);
    setCount(increment);
  }
  return <main><p>Count: {count}</p><button onClick={addPair}>Add pair</button></main>;
}
```

Starting at 0, one Add pair click displays Count: 2. The queued calculation is 0→1→2.

This changed handler passes count +1 twice instead. Both expressions use the same 0 snapshot, so both request replacement by 1. One click displays Count: 1, not 2.

```jsx
function increment(previous) {
  return previous + 1;
}
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);
  function addPair() {
    setCount(count + 1);
    setCount(count + 1);
  }
  return <main><p>Count: {count}</p><button onClick={addPair}>Add pair</button></main>;
}
```
